/// `handles:` answers whether an exception is an instance of the handler's
/// class or of a subclass of it, compared by declaration, so that a nested
/// exception class catches its instances under any enclosing object. A
/// declaration without a body is the level its last clause adds
/// (newspeak-spec.pdf 6.2), a class of the applied mixin M1 and named for
/// its own declaration (6): so `on: M3` catches M3's instances, and never
/// those of M4, another body-less declaration applying M1, nor those of N,
/// which applies M1 with a body of its own -- as two declarations with
/// bodies are two classes. `on: M1` catches all three, whose chains apply
/// M1. UsesM3 applies M3 with a body: its level is named for M3, so
/// `on: M3` catches it, and `on: UsesM3` catches it alone.
///
/// One line per handler class -- M3, M4, N, M1, UsesM3 -- and on each, an
/// instance of M3, M4, N and UsesM3 signalled under it.
class ABodyLessExceptionClassCatchesItsOwnDeclarationsInstances = Object new () (
  class M1 = Object new () ()

  class M3 = Error new <: M1.

  class M4 = Error new <: M1.

  class N = Error new <: M1 () ()

  class UsesM3 = Error new <: M3 () ()

  public main: platform args: arguments = (
    |
      t = platform transcript.
    |
    {M3. M4. N. M1. UsesM3} do: [:handler |
      {M3. M4. N. UsesM3} do: [:signalled |
        t show: ([
          [
            signalled new signal.
            'none'.
          ] on: handler do: [:error | 'caught'].
        ] on: Error do: [:error | 'missed']).
      ] separatedBy: [
        t show: ' '.
      ].
      t newLine.
    ].
  )
)
